THE CORE WHY
The prompt at the old line 51 instructed the model: amount is the net amount
disbursed to the owner, "NOT the gross rent income figure". But
lib/tax/rental-income.ts#actualRentForFy sums rental_payments.amount and
reports it as GROSS rent, feeding schedule.grossRent. Every email-ingested
payment therefore understated assessable rental income by the whole of the
agent's fees and third-party outgoings — and no test anywhere asserted which
figure that column holds.

The prompt now states the opposite explicitly, names netReceived as a separate
field, and says the two "must never be reported as" each other.

WHY THE FILE WAS SPLIT
The defect lived in a prompt string inside a function that calls a model. There
was no way to assert anything about it without contacting OpenAI, which
workflow.md forbids in tests. Exporting the prompt builder and the response
parser makes the invariant reachable; the model call is the only part left
untested, and it is now a three-line function.

DESIGN NOTES
- Per-field `.catch(null)` rather than a strict schema: one unreadable fee line
  should not discard an otherwise good statement. It does NOT coerce — a model
  returning "$4,400.00" yields null, not a number quietly stripped of its
  comma, because a mangled figure ends up in a tax return. Test covers this.
- Absent fee fields default to null, never 0. Zero would assert "the agent
  charged nothing", a claim the model never made. Test covers this.
- The prompt asks for GST-INCLUSIVE fees as they appear on the statement:
  residential rent is input-taxed, so no GST credit is claimable and the
  GST-inclusive figure is the deductible one.
- The prompt tells the model to report figures exactly as read even when they
  do not reconcile, rather than adjusting one to force a balance. A forced
  balance would hide the very discrepancy the reconciliation is meant to
  surface.
- `today` is a parameter with a default, so buildStatementPrompt is a pure
  function of its inputs and testable without freezing the clock.

